EDITOR PAGE CAP — the same valid document, the two surfaces that open it
========================================================================

Why this log exists. §18's perf probe reported `editor_load = n/a — no page was
painted within 30s` for exactly two of its six fixture shapes. The probe cannot
say whether that is a slow load or a refusal, because it stops asking at 30s.
Both shapes turned out to be the many-page ones, and neither is slow.

  fixture                    bytes       pages   perf row
  manypage-fixture.pdf       106,551     300     many-page (104 KB)
  large-fixture.pdf          23,325,047  340     large (22778 KB)
  (the shapes that DID paint: tiny 2 KB and ordinary 5 KB, at 239 ms / 358 ms)

Both are valid: `pdfinfo` reads them, the upload route accepted them, and §6's
runtime matrix compressed the 340-page one with ghostscript. The editor's
MAX_OPEN_PAGES is 200 (lib/editor/loadPdf.ts), so refusing them is BY DESIGN —
the editor rasterises every page up front and will not silently drop pages,
because export draws onto copies of the original.

---- BEFORE THE FIX (HEAD 79e292e) ----

WORKSPACE editor, /workspaces/:id/documents/:id, the 300-page document, polled
every 2s for 240s (node /tmp/manypage-editor.mjs, 120 samples, all identical):

  14s  painted=0 imgs=0 canvases=0
       status=["", "We couldn't open this PDFThe file may be damaged,
       unsupported, or outside the Editor's supported limits", "",
       "No selection", "Select tool active",
       "Could not open document. We couldn't open this PDF."]
  ...
  236s painted=0   (unchanged)
  NEVER PAINTED within 240s

STANDALONE editor, /editor, the SAME file through the real file input
(node /tmp/standalone-cap.mjs):

  attach: true
  2s  → "This PDF has 300 pages. The editor supports up to 200 pages —
        please split the PDF first.Dismiss"

So one surface named the limit and the remedy in 2s; the other called an intact
file possibly damaged, named no number, and — `canRetryKind("invalid-pdf")`
being false and "Open another PDF" being standalone-only — offered the Workspace
user no action at all. The guidance was on the error object the whole time, in
`detail`, which the panel deliberately does not read: `documentLoadState.ts`
authors every string a user sees and passes no text through.

Classification: PRODUCT FAILURE, P2. The refusal is correct; what the user is
told about it is not, on the surface where their documents actually live.

---- THE FIX ----

`PdfOpenError` now carries `{ pages, max }` as NUMBERS beside its message,
`WorkspaceDocumentLoadError` propagates them, `LoadErrorFacts.pageCap` reads
them structurally, and `presentLoadError` composes one authored sentence from
them. Numbers cross the no-pass-through boundary that text could not. Both
surfaces now read that one sentence — the standalone path no longer shows
`err.message`, so the two cannot drift apart again.

An invalid PDF with no cap evidence keeps the generic copy: nothing more
specific is known about it, and inventing a remedy would be worse.

Pinned by lib/editor/loadWorkspaceDocument.test.ts — the test that used to
assert the OLD behaviour (`expect(shown.description).toMatch(/damaged|…/)`),
rewritten to assert both contexts name both numbers, say "split", and never say
"damaged", plus the no-evidence case that must still say it.

---- AFTER THE FIX ----
(filled in below from the rebuilt artifact)

Rebuilt (`npm run build`, `.next` moved aside first so the artifact is clean) and
re-measured against the standalone server, same two probes, same fixture:

  STANDALONE  /editor, attach, 2s:
    "It has 300 pages, and the Editor supports up to 200. Split it into smaller
     files first — the Split PDF tool does this."

  WORKSPACE   /workspaces/:id/documents/:id, first sample at 2s and every sample
              after it:
    heading      "This PDF has too many pages to edit"
    description  "It has 300 pages, and the Editor supports up to 200. Sp…"
    live region  "Could not open document. This PDF has too many pages to edit."

The document still does not open, which is correct: MAX_OPEN_PAGES is a real
resource bound and rasterising 300 pages up front is what it exists to prevent.
What changed is that the user is now told the truth about why, on both surfaces,
in one sentence authored in one place.

---- A NOTE ON THE PROBE THAT FOUND THIS ----

`editorLoad` in scripts/perf-load-probe.mjs reported this as
`n/a — no page was painted within 30s`. That is the harness stating its own blind
spot as a property of the product: the refusal arrives in about two seconds, and
nothing was waiting for thirty. It now reads the editor's own error panel when
the paint never comes, so the §18 table distinguishes "slow" from "declined".

---- A PROCESS NOTE, because it cost a diagnosis ----

Between the two measurements the first rebuild appeared to break the editor
entirely: `/editor` answered 200 but `/_next/static/chunks/3kesmnhug2u93.js`
answered 500, and the editor's error boundary replaced the canvas. It was not a
product defect and not the build. `scripts/restart-origin.sh` uses
`exec node server.js` from inside `.next/standalone`, so the process is named
`next-server` and `pkill -f ".next/standalone/server.js"` matches nothing — the
PREVIOUS origin was still holding 127.0.0.1:3002, serving HTML from a build whose
static directory had just been moved out from under it, while the new server died
with EADDRINUSE in a log nobody was reading. Found with
`lsof -nP -iTCP:3002 -sTCP:LISTEN` plus the process's own cwd, stopped by pid,
restarted. The §25 lesson generalises: verify WHICH server answered before
believing what it said.
